WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.382
Hi, welcome back to Nagarjuna.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.360
I made a mistake at the
end of the last class.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.730
I said we're finished with
the chapter, but we're not.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.640
We're still in the same chapter.
I think it's chapter 16,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.640
which is talking about
being chained to sansara

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.257
and then getting nirvana,

00:00:20.257 --> 00:00:24.480
which is releasing the chains
and getting away from sansara.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.350
This second half of this chapter

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.900
doesn't take very long to teach.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.860
So I thought you guys might
like to see something,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.460
you could appreciate,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.420
the work of trying to
translate these texts,

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.620
trying to find these texts.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.620
So you're kind of at the
end of the food chain.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.497
You are speaking of chains.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.810
You are getting all this information,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:02.120
all these teachings
because of other people,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:07.120
who worked so hard to find
the books, catalog the books,

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.790
scan the books, input the
books, save the books.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.170
And I'm talking about ALL,

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.175
all of the hard work they do.

00:01:16.175 --> 00:01:20.730
So my part is the easy part,

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.480
I just explain something.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.550
I get the book because other
people have worked so hard

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.840
for over 30 years to find these books

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.980
and to bring me books.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.860
They're like these guys
that go out in the world,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.490
find the books and they
bring them back home.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.700
I get to sit and read them
and share them with you.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.070
So I kind of wanted to
recognize that huge effort

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:51.513
of John Brady and his whole team of ALL,

00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:53.386
also all the Gold Clubs,

00:01:53.386 --> 00:01:56.550
the "club de oros" around the world,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.230
all of you guys who are
working so hard to raise money.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.393
I think sometimes, if I'm you,

00:02:04.393 --> 00:02:06.520
I would think well what
do they do with the money?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.700
Where's the money go?

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.670
And I thought you would appreciate

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.670
seeing how your donation
became this class.

00:02:16.157 --> 00:02:18.965
So first of all,

00:02:18.965 --> 00:02:23.965
the donations made 20, 22 years ago,

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.390
they went into, we were trying to find

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:33.390
Choney Lama's books. And
they were, you know...

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.220
In those days, it was
very hard to get a copy

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.640
of any of Choney Lama's books.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.850
My teacher showed me
one book of Choney Lama

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.830
and I was like, wow!

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.860
I studied, he has 250 books like this.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.213
Come on, Rinpoche, give them to me.

00:02:51.213 --> 00:02:53.180
He's like, "No, you can't get them."

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.710
And I was like, "What do
you mean we can't get them?"

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.637
He says, "We made a mistake."

00:02:57.637 --> 00:02:58.917
And I'm like "Who's we?"

00:02:58.917 --> 00:03:01.940
He says, "My monastery,
Sera Me, we made a mistake."

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.450
I'm like, "Well, what's the mistake?"

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.440
He says, "We didn't want other monasteries

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.630
to get Choney Lama's books because then

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.200
we could win all the debates
with the other monasteries.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.630
So we kept all of the Choney
Lama's books very carefully."

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:17.900
I'm like, "Well, what happened?"

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.290
He said, "Well, we kept
them in the library."

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.050
I'm like, "What happened to the library?"

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.730
He says, "Well, during
the cultural revolution

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.600
the library was bombed
and it all burned up."

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.560
Every copy burned up.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.920
We made a booboo. We put
all the copies in one place

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.020
nobody else... probably
that's the karma, right?

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:35.880
Nobody else could get them.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.680
Then they all burned up.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.870
Then we ran away.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.580
That's why I'm teaching you right now.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.580
I'm like, so we started
looking for Choney Lama's,

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.410
any copies of Choney Lama's books

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:53.810
and finally Jason Dunbar,
who was a great ALL,

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:58.590
early worker for ALL, he went to Tibet

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.280
and he went to Choney
monastery and he begged

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.280
for scans of Choney Lama's
books and he got them.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.933
And he got all of them, and
I'd like to show you one here.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:15.680
And, you know, in those days,

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.613
copy machines in east
Tibet, Choney district,

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:22.475
they weren't too good,

00:04:22.475 --> 00:04:24.870
but you can see it's
a pretty cool carving.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:28.130
And that's what it looks like.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:33.130
The end paper, the sideways
letters on the left side,

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.400
they say @RTZA...

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.307
which means RTZA BA SHES RAB KYI @TI KA

00:04:40.307 --> 00:04:42.782
RTZA BA SHES RAB KYI TI KA

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.373
TI KA means commentary,

00:04:44.373 --> 00:04:46.940
and RTZA BA SHES RAB
is root text on Wisdom.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:51.320
So you find the name of
the book is at the end,

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.280
and then the page number is there which is

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.940
@... BDUN, sorry,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.250
yeah, @...TRUS DUN which means 67.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.637
And then there'll be a volume
number, which is probably LNGA.

00:05:06.703 --> 00:05:09.520
And that means his collected
works are multiple volumes.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.770
So if the page gets
separated from the book,

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.310
which often happens because
they're not bound together,

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.990
then you can find the
book and put it back in.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.260
Because teachers are always
giving pages to their students

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.530
and say memorize that, give
me back the page tomorrow,

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.442
and they never do.

00:05:25.442 --> 00:05:30.442
And so there's the copy
from Choney monastery,

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.780
and it was the only
copy that we could find.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.180
And so anytime we had a question

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.593
we didn't have any place to check.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.220
We didn't have anything to compare it to,

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.190
there was no other
carving available to us.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.530
And it often happens
that you have a question

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.970
about a carving. Is the
carving correct or not?

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:56.430
So then we ended up going to Russia.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:01.430
We found, I don't know,
141,000 Tibetan manuscripts

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.607
in the collection of the king.

00:06:04.607 --> 00:06:08.970
The Czar of Russia had a
collection that he collected,

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.230
he was a book collector.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:14.507
Russians are inveterate book
collectors, they love books,

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:17.380
and he collected 141,000 manuscripts.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:19.410
And we found a second copy.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.083
So here's a, you can
see in the next picture,

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.310
this is a copy of Choney Lama's work.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:30.790
Much better carving, as you
can see, and more clear.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.682
It took 12 years

00:06:35.682 --> 00:06:38.174
to catalog those books.

00:06:38.174 --> 00:06:41.640
It took 12 years not to
copy them, not to scan them,

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.920
it took 12 years to write the names down.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.350
And we had three guys working full-time.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.720
And then once we saw,

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.416
in the contract, we had something
in the contract that said

00:06:56.416 --> 00:07:00.500
we can make copies of
our monastery's books,

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.620
and we got permission to make copies.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.320
Let me see that copy again.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.400
Now, if the guy holding the paper down

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.460
while the roller is going
across the ink on the woodblock

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:17.270
looks up at some, probably
not a girl, but something,

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:22.020
and then he'll get these
big stripes down the page,

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.810
you see on the right side,
there's a blank spot?

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.320
And then, you know,
Geshe-la has to try to guess

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.160
what was there on the woodblock,

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.690
and it's really, really
difficult sometimes.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.690
So it's very important to
have multiple woodblocks.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.830
It's very, very important
to go around the world

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:45.030
and try to find another copy.

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:49.080
And it takes money to fly
from East Tibet to Russia.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.630
It takes money to talk
Russians into letting you

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.730
work on their books for 12 years,

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.080
and that's what you guys
are fundraising for.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.010
It takes a lot of money.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.540
It has, I would say 5 to
10 million dollars so far,

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:04.110
and we're about 5% finished.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:08.900
So, you know, if we want
to have these classes

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.380
and if we want to be
released from the chains

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:13.080
that bind us to sansara,

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.983
whoever it is that is bound to sansara,

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.270
we need these books,
and then we need money.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:24.340
It costs money to fly around
and go into ancient libraries

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:26.310
and spend months and months, years,

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.100
12 years, it takes money.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.810
Those guys who lived in St. Petersburg...

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:34.393
St. Petersburg is the most
expensive European city

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.140
because you can't grow anything there.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.510
When I went there they said

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:40.735
don't spend much money on dinner.

00:08:40.735 --> 00:08:42.980
And so I said okay.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:46.930
And so I went to dinner, I
said all I want is a salad.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.120
And they gave me a salad and
the next day I said okay,

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:50.950
I didn't spend much money on dinner.

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:51.783
- What'd you buy?

00:08:51.783 --> 00:08:52.760
- I bought a salad.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.930
- Oh my God, that's the
most expensive thing!

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:58.000
You know, that's like $50.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.230
I'm like why?

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.740
They said, "You're in
Russia, it's too cold,

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.100
you can't grow vegetables here, duh!

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.447
You should've had some sheep
meat or something [laughs].

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:11.450
So it costs money to stay
there, it costs money to,

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.870
a lot of money to stay there for 12 years.

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:16.390
And also all kinds of problems.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:18.460
We got, one of our staff got kidnapped

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.530
by the mafia in Russia.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.490
They said 50,000 bucks or
we're going to kill him,

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.753
and you know, that's expensive.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.900
So anyway, one more
thing I want to show you

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.060
of why John Brady is so brilliant

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.780
and why his project
deserves your help so much,

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:37.090
let's see the next slide.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.280
Now this is the woodblock
from Choney monastery.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.160
Probably it was printed
sometime around 1700,

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.280
I mean carved around 1700.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:52.646
And if you know Tibetan,

00:09:55.105 --> 00:09:57.080
look at the fourth line, okay Tim?

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.632
You want to read it slowly?

00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:01.505
From GZHAN.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:04.960
Come on!

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:09.960
RGYU DANG 'BRAS BU

00:10:11.568 --> 00:10:13.460
NYID DANG

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:14.745
Keep going.

00:10:14.745 --> 00:10:16.353
DANG NI

00:10:17.318 --> 00:10:21.051
MTSAN GZHI

00:10:22.251 --> 00:10:24.064
NYID DANG

00:10:24.064 --> 00:10:25.843
Okay, good.

00:10:25.843 --> 00:10:30.669
It says 'BRAS BU NYID
DANG NI MTSAN GZHI NYID

00:10:30.669 --> 00:10:31.910
And Geshe-la is translating,

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.310
Geshe-la's preparing for this class.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:37.550
And Geshe-la's going crazy
because it doesn't make sense.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.540
There's something wrong here.

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.230
Like Geshe-la, after many, many years,

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.620
you smell something's missing.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.500
Like there's something's missing here.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.410
And by the way, Tim,
you can appreciate it.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:54.750
He's got pairs here, he's
got members of pairs.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.960
He's got RGYU and 'BRAS
BU, cause and effect.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.360
Later he's going to have
TSOR BA and TSOR BA PO,

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:05.170
the feeling and the feeler.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.870
And then between that you've got

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.880
thing defined with no definition.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.220
You've got thing defined,
it's a list of pairs,

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.930
but there's one single guy in the middle.

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:21.520
It should have, what word is missing?

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.190
This is what is defined,
we need the word for?

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.293
Definition, MTSAN NYID.

00:11:28.293 --> 00:11:30.400
MTSAN NYID is missing here.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.850
So Geshe-la spends three hours,

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.890
he struggles, struggle,
struggle, struggle,

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.400
and then he says the goddamn block,

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:37.910
there's something wrong with the block.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:40.910
That guy was also looking up at something

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:44.323
and didn't carve definition.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:47.220
In the block, you know?

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.703
So what does Geshe-la do?

00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:52.410
Yeah, I got to go find the other block,

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:54.550
but if ALL hasn't had the money

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.260
to fly to St. Petersburg
and stay there for 12 years,

00:11:57.260 --> 00:11:59.710
I won't have a second block, got it?

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:01.600
That's why we need the Gold Club,

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.900
and that's why you guys have
to support the Gold Club.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:08.900
Even $5, even $1, if there's enough mice,

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:12.970
they can hold up the Titanic.
[students laughing]

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.610
That's the whole story
with the gold clubs.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.900
We're not, it's not big, rich people,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:23.590
it's a bunch of small
people who give $1, $5, $10,

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:25.500
and then we hold up the Titanic.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.990
So I want to play with you.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:31.590
Geshe-la decided there's
something missing from the block,

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.140
Geshe-la went to the second
block, let's go see it.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.440
By the way, I did this
in the car this morning.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:42.283
Now, Tim, read the third line.

00:12:44.889 --> 00:12:48.806
MTSAN NYID DANG MTSAN GZHI NYID

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:51.823
Yeah, what's there?

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:56.713
The word that Geshe-la
predicted should be there.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.400
There's definition, MTSAN
NYID and MTSAN GZHI.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.215
Go back, go back, make it big.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.993
Now, $100.

00:13:11.384 --> 00:13:15.230
How the hell did they
change the woodblock?

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:17.900
Somebody corrected it like 200 years ago.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:21.180
Somebody sat there like
Geshe-la for three hours

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.860
and said something's wrong here,
and then they corrected it.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:27.777
But how do you correct a woodblock?

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:33.740
You shave off the letters
and you dig up a square spot,

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:39.310
then you take a piece of wood
and make it the same size

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:43.020
and you glue it into the
block and then you recarve it.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:45.170
But there's no room to recarve it

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.880
because we're adding a
word, so what do you do?

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.010
You shrink everything!

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:53.180
Look at the third line, it's very cool.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:54.700
You shrink everything.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:58.154
He's trying, the guy's
trying to carve, you know...

00:13:58.154 --> 00:14:00.978
- Hey, could you just
fit in two more words?

00:14:00.978 --> 00:14:02.570
- What?

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:03.860
- Come on, you can do it.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:06.000
And you can see this guy
sitting there carving.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.090
It would take about, I don't know,

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:10.440
three or four hours to carve it.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.690
And he's carving everything smaller,

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:16.830
so if you look at the
picture you can see that.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:20.820
It's very cool to me,
some guy took the block,

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:25.410
cut out the missing part, and glued it in.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.340
And that's why it's smaller, got it, cool?

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:30.510
Isn't that cool?

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.580
Now, if Gold Club doesn't send guys

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.773
to St. Petersburg library,

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:39.120
then I cannot explain the book to you

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.900
because there's something
wrong with the book, got it?

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.040
And then it's wrong forever.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.650
If we don't fix it, trust me,

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:50.009
no one else cares about it [laughs].

00:14:50.009 --> 00:14:50.842
All right?

00:14:50.842 --> 00:14:52.010
So I think it's cool.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.820
All right, now let's go
to the impossible book.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:58.180
The book's already impossible,
but if there's words missing,

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:01.859
it gets a little more difficult [laughs].

00:15:01.859 --> 00:15:03.457
All right, here we go.

00:15:03.457 --> 00:15:04.840
,GAL TE NYE BAR LEN 'CHING NA,

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:06.448
,NYE BAR LEN BCAS 'CHING MI 'GYUR,

00:15:06.448 --> 00:15:09.206
,NYE BAR LEN MED MI 'CHING STE,

00:15:09.206 --> 00:15:11.322
,GNAS SKABS GANG ZHIG 'CHING BAR 'GYUR,

00:15:11.322 --> 00:15:13.870
Don't forget we're talking
about being chained.

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:15.880
'CHING BA means "chained", right?

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:19.463
Chained, who's chained
to what, by the way?

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:22.609
We are chained to what?

00:15:22.609 --> 00:15:23.540
[student responding indistinctly]

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:26.863
Mind and body which are, oops, messed up.

00:15:28.089 --> 00:15:30.810
Okay, we're chained to a
messed up body and mind.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:33.340
We are chained to a mortal body

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:36.590
and we are chained to an f-ed up mind.

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:39.573
Okay? [laughs] That's the chains, got it?

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:42.301
- Who's chained?

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.300
- Michael.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.830
Okay, Michael is chained to
a messed up body and mind.

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:50.700
Okay.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:52.450
- Is Michael the chain?

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.020
- No, I mean, you chain somebody

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.550
to a wall in a dungeon, right?

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:00.460
You chain a prisoner to the wall.

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.480
Is the chain part of the wall,

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:05.563
or is the chain part of the prisoner?

00:16:07.084 --> 00:16:08.620
- Neither, I mean it's between

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:11.280
the prisoner and the wall, right?

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:12.740
Got it?

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:15.690
So, and by the way, in the Abhidharma,

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:18.560
the chain is a separate entity.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:19.840
It's very interesting.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:24.480
They say there's your body and
mind and then there's Pachi,

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:27.630
and the chain is some kind
of mystical third thing

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:32.421
that keeps Pachi chained
to his body and mind.

00:16:32.421 --> 00:16:35.330
And Nagarjuna's like come on, you know.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:39.110
Either the chain is part
of the body and mind,

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:40.470
or the chain is part of the guy

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.370
who's chained to the body and mind.

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:45.210
But the chain, where else
is the chain going to be?

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.677
The guy dies, what happens,
the chain just sits around

00:16:47.677 --> 00:16:50.592
and the whole universe
is filled with chains?

00:16:50.592 --> 00:16:51.930
You know?

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.340
Who's getting chained?

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:55.510
Who's chained?

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:58.971
And then, I'm going to
skip to this last line.

00:16:58.971 --> 00:17:01.887
SONG DANG MA SONG BGOM PAS BSTAN

00:17:01.887 --> 00:17:04.781
SONG DANG MA SONG BGOM PAS BSTAN

00:17:04.781 --> 00:17:09.680
$100, BGOM, $100.

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:13.391
Yeah, he's correct, it's not meditation.

00:17:13.391 --> 00:17:14.370
[student speaking indistinctly]

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:17.550
No, nothing to do with meditation.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.589
We learned it like 100 times, but.

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:22.880
[student speaking indistinctly]

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:26.199
Very similar spelling and
exactly the same pronunciation

00:17:26.199 --> 00:17:27.349
because it has a prefix.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:34.970
For $50 I'll give you a clue.
[students laughing]

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:37.640
It came before.

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:43.277
It came in the second chapter, $25.

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:50.620
Walking, yay, 25 bucks for Gyelse, yay!

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:53.279
Awesome.

00:17:53.279 --> 00:17:54.660
All right, oh you cheated, okay.

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:57.162
That's all right, don't tell me.

00:17:57.162 --> 00:17:58.360
Yeah, walking.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:00.727
What's that got to do with chains?

00:18:00.727 --> 00:18:01.860
$100.

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.350
[student responding indistinctly]

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:05.790
Yeah, wait, he's showing me.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:06.960
Yeah, here.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.340
Where's the...

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:11.650
[students responding indistinctly]

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:14.650
Yeah, where's the part?
There's a part of the path

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:18.990
that you walked, there's
a part of the path you

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:21.130
haven't walked yet.

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:24.450
The part of the path
that you're walking on

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.410
is supposed to be somewhere between,

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:27.670
but you can't define it,

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:32.670
and that's very similar
to the idea of the chains.

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:37.160
The middle point, the part of the path

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:39.328
that you're stepping on now, BGOM.

00:18:39.328 --> 00:18:41.270
because BGOM is always active.

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:42.730
Stepping, it's not really walking

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:46.060
it's putting your foot down.

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:47.660
Where's the part of your path,

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.805
where is the exact part of the
path of the present moment?

00:18:50.805 --> 00:18:53.040
[vocalizing]

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:54.720
Where is the present moment?

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.095
That's where the chain is.

00:18:59.863 --> 00:19:01.820
Got it? It's cool, right?

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:03.104
Where is the chain?

00:19:03.104 --> 00:19:07.797
Oh, it's like halfway between
you and your body and mind.

00:19:07.797 --> 00:19:09.177
[laughs]

00:19:09.177 --> 00:19:12.063
So Nagarjuna's like get it?

00:19:12.063 --> 00:19:15.360
Halfway between you
and your body and mind.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.663
So let's do the language here.

00:19:18.981 --> 00:19:20.744
GAL TE NYE BAR LEN 'CHING NA

00:19:20.744 --> 00:19:25.744
If you are chained to your body and mind,

00:19:27.126 --> 00:19:29.420
NYE BAR LEN BCAS 'CHING MI 'GYUR,

00:19:29.420 --> 00:19:31.777
then you're not chained.

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:38.013
If the chain is part of your body and mind

00:19:38.013 --> 00:19:40.420
then the chain's not part of you.

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:41.875
How's that?

00:19:41.875 --> 00:19:45.780
NYE BAR LEN MED MI 'CHING STE

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:49.570
Then, if you let go of your body and mind,

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:51.687
you wouldn't be chained anymore.

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.070
If you died you wouldn't
be chained anymore,

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:56.300
you'd go to Nirvana.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:59.320
If the chain is part of your
messed up body and mind,

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:03.030
then when you drop a body
and mind, as you keep doing,

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:05.805
then you should be unchained for awhile.

00:20:06.814 --> 00:20:08.650
GNAS SKABS GANG ZHIG 'CHING BAR 'GYUR

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:11.323
Then what point is it
where you're chained?

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:16.150
Now, same argument, you
tell me where it comes from.

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:20.193
For $100, god you guys are
making so much money today.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:22.930
Ready?

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.639
Everybody's like...

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:30.333
Did the chains exist
before the guy was chained?

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:37.130
Now, what's the $100, Geshe-la?

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:39.090
You take me back to a previous verse

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:40.783
in this series of classes.

00:20:41.815 --> 00:20:43.615
[student responding indistinctly]

00:20:43.615 --> 00:20:47.260
That's good, but we were
talking before that.

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:50.690
By the way, the ninth chapter's name

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.230
is "Was There Something There Before".

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:57.990
The ninth chapter's
name is an investigation

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:00.023
of whether something was there before.

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:04.078
And it referred to one of the senses.

00:21:04.078 --> 00:21:05.128
[student responding indistinctly]

00:21:05.128 --> 00:21:07.870
Yeah, when you look at something...

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:10.300
Yeah, is the eye there?

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:13.253
Is the little girl with
the telescope there?

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:19.470
She walks outside and
then the stars are there?

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:22.310
Does the perceiver come first
or the seeing comes first?

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:24.310
Which one?

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:26.710
Does the little girl walk
out with the telescope

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:29.830
and then looks at the
stars, or how does it work?

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:32.860
Does the little girl with the telescope

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:35.983
walk out of her house and
then look at the stars?

00:21:36.995 --> 00:21:39.430
[student responding indistinctly]

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.580
She has to, she has to because
there has to be a telescope

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.633
before there's astronomy.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:50.430
But she can't because
she's not an astronomer

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:52.180
till she looks at the stars.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:53.790
So the whole thing's impossible.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:56.100
Now take that here.

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:58.140
Chains, did the chains come first,

00:21:58.140 --> 00:21:59.566
or the chain-ee came first?

00:21:59.566 --> 00:22:02.000
[student responding indistinctly]

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:03.230
Yeah, good, you can't be a chain-ee

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:05.130
until you've been chained.

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:08.160
But you've got to have
chains to be chained first.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.833
Okay, the whole thing
is something messed up.

00:22:13.457 --> 00:22:17.115
And the point is,

00:22:17.115 --> 00:22:18.815
if there's trouble in the kitchen

00:22:18.815 --> 00:22:21.320
figuring out why the husband's upset,

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.960
you have to look for other options.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:25.850
Maybe it's all coming from you.

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:28.025
Maybe it's all coming from you.

00:22:28.025 --> 00:22:29.730
Okay, that's the bottom line.

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:31.590
Maybe it's all coming from you.

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:34.440
Maybe you make the telescope
and then you make the girl

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:37.440
and you make the astronomy.

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:38.730
All coming from you.

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.948
But they are not causing
each other, out there.

00:22:41.948 --> 00:22:43.400
[vocalizing]

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:47.548
It looks like [vocalizing],
but it's really [vocalizing].

00:22:47.548 --> 00:22:48.800
Okay, got it?

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:50.396
That's all.

00:22:50.396 --> 00:22:52.210
If you realize it's [vocalizing],

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:54.160
then you can do Medicine Buddha mantra.

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:59.949
But if you think you're
[vocalizing], you're in trouble.

00:22:59.949 --> 00:23:02.240
If you're out there doing
Medicine Buddha mantras,

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:03.420
you're in trouble.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:05.690
If you realize that the
mantra comes from you,

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:08.850
the illness comes from you,
the fixing comes from you,

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:12.160
then you can use a mantra, cool?

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:14.810
Yay, let's go to the next one.

00:23:14.810 --> 00:23:18.143
Oh, by the way, next picture.

00:23:18.143 --> 00:23:20.843
Is the guy shackled or not?

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:28.893
Wait, no, the guy before that.

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:32.220
[laughs]

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:34.890
Is this guy shackled?

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:37.100
No, because there's no chains yet.

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:39.692
Therefore the chains have
to come first, right?

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:42.455
Nevermind.

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:44.913
Next verse.

00:23:46.797 --> 00:23:48.847
RE ZHIG BCINGS PA

00:23:51.172 --> 00:23:52.472
MI GROL TE,

00:23:52.472 --> 00:23:55.778
MA BCINGS PA YANG GROL MI 'GYUR,

00:23:55.778 --> 00:23:56.611
Okay.

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:00.680
This is very subtle.

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:03.630
It says...

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:07.230
RE ZHIG means provisionally,

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:10.818
for the time being, for now.

00:24:10.818 --> 00:24:12.709
RE ZHIG -- for now.

00:24:12.709 --> 00:24:14.460
BCINGS PA MI GROL TE

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:18.333
You cannot take off your chains.

00:24:19.408 --> 00:24:23.160
Let's look, it's kind of like look.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:26.393
Look, you can't take off your chains. Why?

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:30.113
First of all, he's like
that, first of all,

00:24:30.113 --> 00:24:33.530
Nagarjuna, first of all, you
cannot take off your chains,

00:24:33.530 --> 00:24:35.855
you cannot take off these chains.

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:45.271
You cannot not fight with your husband.

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:53.130
You cannot take off these chains. Why?

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:55.580
Because you see the chains.

00:24:56.589 --> 00:24:59.950
You cannot take off the chains. Why?

00:24:59.950 --> 00:25:04.660
Because you think those
chains are coming this way.

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:09.013
Then you, the way you think
about the chains is the chain.

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:11.490
Got it?

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:14.690
You can't, he just says, "Hey
let me tell you something."

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:15.523
- What?

00:25:15.523 --> 00:25:18.010
- You can't take these chains off.

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:18.843
- Why?

00:25:18.843 --> 00:25:22.810
- Because these chains
are coming this way.

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:27.440
And thinking they're coming
this way is the real chains.

00:25:27.440 --> 00:25:29.640
It's very sexy.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:31.620
Thinking the chains are coming this way

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:34.430
when you look at the
chains is the real chain,

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:35.670
and if you look at the chains

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:38.610
then you're going to be
chained more and more.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.090
You guys, okay, got it?

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:42.567
Very sexy.

00:25:42.567 --> 00:25:44.590
MA BCINGS PA YANG GROL MI 'GYUR

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:45.990
Even if I took the chains off you,

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:48.273
you would still be chained [laughs].

00:25:49.986 --> 00:25:52.758
Okay, got it?

00:25:52.758 --> 00:25:55.449
BCINGS PA GROL BZHIN YIN 'GYUR NA

00:25:55.449 --> 00:25:57.949
BCINGS DANG GROL BA DUS GCIG 'GYUR

00:25:57.949 --> 00:26:00.490
If there was a middle
point in the stepping

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:03.100
of the foot on the path,

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:06.183
between being chained and unchained,

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:12.120
then in the same, then somewhere,

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:14.670
the part of the path where you have been

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:19.330
and the part of the path where
you will be have to touch.

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:20.840
If there's a place between

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:23.410
chained and unchained that can touch,

00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:26.460
then you can be chained and
unchained in the same moment.

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:29.100
It's not possible.

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:32.929
Like you can see Nagarjuna going...

00:26:32.929 --> 00:26:35.385
Okay, we can make a dance, right?

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:38.910
Some kind of Hawaiian thing.

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:43.918
Okay, next verse.

00:26:47.767 --> 00:26:49.775
,BDAG NI LEN MED MYA NGAN 'DA',

00:26:49.775 --> 00:26:52.675
,MYANG 'DAS BDAG GIR 'GYUR RO ZHES,

00:26:55.550 --> 00:26:57.180
Let's just start with the first two lines,

00:26:57.180 --> 00:26:59.873
because this gets heavy,
this gets difficult.

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:05.210
BDAG NI LEN MED MYA NGAN 'DA'

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:07.481
If I could not @NYE BAR LEN PA,

00:27:07.481 --> 00:27:10.360
but that would be MYA NGAN LAS 'DAS PA.

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:12.287
If I could stop @NYE BAR LEN PA-ing,

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:15.510
I could reach Nirvana.

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:17.922
What's @NYE BAR LEN PA?

00:27:17.922 --> 00:27:18.755
[student responding indistinctly]

00:27:18.755 --> 00:27:22.370
Yeah, if I could stop taking
on new bodies and minds,

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:24.290
that's Nirvana, you know?

00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:29.290
If I could stop taking
on cancer filled bodies

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:34.177
and anger filled minds,
that would be Nirvana,

00:27:34.177 --> 00:27:36.377
you know, if I could stop that.

00:27:36.377 --> 00:27:38.910
MYANG 'DAS BDAG GIR 'GYUR RO ZHES

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:42.533
I would like to make Nirvana my own.

00:27:43.489 --> 00:27:46.420
BDAG 'GYUR

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:50.420
I would like to make Nirvana my own.

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:51.610
Then you're already in trouble,

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:54.190
because me, my, me and my.

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:56.920
That's the definition
of the misunderstanding

00:27:56.920 --> 00:27:59.217
which is which link of the wheel of life?

00:27:59.217 --> 00:28:03.070
[student responding indistinctly]

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:05.857
The blind man, which link?

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:10.410
Number one, causes everything else.

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:13.240
The minute you say I want Nirvana,

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:14.473
then you're already in trouble.

00:28:14.473 --> 00:28:16.440
Who's me? Who's I?

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:17.959
What Nirvana?

00:28:17.959 --> 00:28:20.730
BDAG GIR ZHES means

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:23.110
I want to make it mine.

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:28.110
But, Christina, the word for mine in...

00:28:29.632 --> 00:28:31.898
the word for me "atman"

00:28:33.037 --> 00:28:34.263
also means

00:28:35.524 --> 00:28:37.150
[student responding indistinctly]

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:40.030
self nature, self nature.

00:28:40.030 --> 00:28:44.485
When we say BDAG MED, "anatman", anatman,

00:28:44.485 --> 00:28:45.760
no self nature.

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:49.690
The word for "me" in Tibetan and Sanskrit

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:52.563
is the word for self existence.

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:57.220
So when Nagarjuna says,
hey, what do you want to do?

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:58.370
- I want to reach Nirvana.

00:28:58.370 --> 00:28:59.403
- What do you want to do to Nirvana?

00:28:59.403 --> 00:29:01.140
- I want to make it mine.

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:04.400
Oh yeah, self existing mine, yeah, cool.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.206
Got it?

00:29:06.206 --> 00:29:07.039
Atman.

00:29:07.039 --> 00:29:09.170
I want the atman to own the Nirvana.

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:10.320
But atman don't exist

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.760
because atman means self nature.

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:15.426
All right, DE LTAR GANG DAG 'DZIN DE YI.

00:29:15.426 --> 00:29:18.480
NYER LEN 'DZIN PA CHEN PO YIN

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:22.120
Your desire to make Nirvana your own

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:25.943
is just an advanced form
of misunderstanding.

00:29:26.860 --> 00:29:27.693
Whoa.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:33.770
Your desire to make Nirvana my own

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:37.199
is link number one in the
goddamn wheel of life!

00:29:37.199 --> 00:29:39.260
Duh, okay!

00:29:39.260 --> 00:29:40.550
- I want to get out of the wheel of life.

00:29:40.550 --> 00:29:42.030
- Yeah, okay, how?

00:29:42.030 --> 00:29:43.560
- I'm going to make Nirvana my own.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:46.201
- Yeah, cool, that's the first
link in the wheel of life.

00:29:46.201 --> 00:29:48.925
[laughs] Got it?

00:29:48.925 --> 00:29:51.122
You should be laughing, I
don't know what's going on.

00:29:51.122 --> 00:29:53.040
Let's do it again.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:54.420
- I want to get out of the wheel of life.

00:29:54.420 --> 00:29:56.260
- Yeah, really? What are you going to do?

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:58.390
- I'm going to make Nirvana my own.

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:00.180
Oh yeah, that's the first
link in the wheel of life,

00:30:00.180 --> 00:30:02.387
very good, very good [laughs].

00:30:02.387 --> 00:30:03.400
Okay, got it?

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:05.308
It's kind of funny.

00:30:07.431 --> 00:30:11.572
GANG LA MYA NGAN 'DAS BSKYED MED

00:30:11.572 --> 00:30:14.947
'KHOR BA BSAL BA'ANG YOD MIN PA

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:21.730
This is very difficult
and you need Choney Lama.

00:30:21.730 --> 00:30:24.305
The line says

00:30:26.222 --> 00:30:29.940
nowhere could you create Nirvana.

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:34.080
Nowhere could you BSKYED. This
is the D, BSKYED with the D

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:36.620
which means "to create"
as opposed to "be born".

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:40.820
Without the D -- "be born",
with the D -- "create".

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:44.960
What's the most famous use of this verb?

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:46.460
That's pretty close.

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:47.830
That's actually true.

00:30:47.830 --> 00:30:49.697
How about SEMS BSKYED?

00:30:50.922 --> 00:30:52.010
To give birth to the mind,

00:30:52.010 --> 00:30:54.690
to give birth to the
wish for enlightenment.

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:59.100
So, but here, okay, $100 question.

00:30:59.100 --> 00:31:01.760
God, you guys are making
so much money today.

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:07.677
Choney Lama says... the word BSKYED

00:31:07.677 --> 00:31:09.700
here, which means to create,

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:11.830
"You want to create your Nirvana, right?"

00:31:11.830 --> 00:31:16.690
You want to bring, you want
to create your Nirvana.

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:20.010
He says the word create
here is a code word

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:21.500
for SGRO 'DOGS

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:26.079
in that pair called SGRO
'DOGS and SKUR 'DEBS.

00:31:26.079 --> 00:31:27.817
- [Jigme] Overestimation.

00:31:27.817 --> 00:31:29.180
- Yay, $100!

00:31:29.180 --> 00:31:31.480
Jigme, can you pay Jigme $100?

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:33.400
Now which Jigme takes the money

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:35.668
and which Jigme gives the money?

00:31:35.668 --> 00:31:38.388
And where's the line between?

00:31:38.388 --> 00:31:41.890
Yeah, overrate.

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:43.716
So what's it mean here?

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:47.520
When you say I want to make Nirvana mine,

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:51.343
you are creating a fake Nirvana.

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:55.070
You are creating a self existent Nirvana.

00:31:55.070 --> 00:31:58.640
When you say I want to make Nirvana mine,

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:03.210
you are creating Nirvana.
And then Choney Lama says,

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:05.440
"When Nagarjuna says create here,

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:08.523
he means a self existent Nirvana, got it?

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:13.597
Okay, 'KHOR BA BSAL BA'ANG YOD MIN PA.

00:32:16.124 --> 00:32:17.512
'KHOR BA BSAL BA means

00:32:17.512 --> 00:32:21.904
you couldn't also remove that Nirvana.

00:32:21.904 --> 00:32:23.480
BSAL BA here,

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:25.490
which before meant "ajate",

00:32:25.490 --> 00:32:27.112
for $100?

00:32:27.112 --> 00:32:28.407
- [Woman] Drive.

00:32:28.407 --> 00:32:30.563
- Damn, $100!

00:32:31.430 --> 00:32:32.660
To drive.

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:34.920
Before, BSAL BA

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:39.340
meant "to drive", right,
the existence of a thing.

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:42.860
Here it means BSAL BA
like clear light BSAL BA.

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:45.223
To remove.

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:50.540
If you try to make a Nirvana
yours which is self existent,

00:32:50.540 --> 00:32:54.560
that would be also a Nirvana
that would always be the same

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:56.772
and could never be real.

00:32:56.772 --> 00:33:00.532
DE LA 'KHOR BA CI ZHIG YIN

00:33:00.532 --> 00:33:03.000
What do you think Nirvana is?

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:04.963
What do you think Nirvana is?

00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:07.823
That's, who's asking?

00:33:07.823 --> 00:33:09.470
CI ZHIG

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:14.470
I'm sorry, you guys who
are creating a Nirvana,

00:33:15.330 --> 00:33:17.063
what do you think sansara is?

00:33:17.063 --> 00:33:18.492
What do you think?

00:33:18.492 --> 00:33:19.580
What do you think Sansara is?

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:22.980
Now, you want to know
some frustrating thing?

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:25.473
The answer is missing from both carvings.

00:33:25.473 --> 00:33:28.280
[students laughing]

00:33:28.280 --> 00:33:31.030
The answer is missing from both carvings.

00:33:31.030 --> 00:33:33.160
It's amazing.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:37.583
Nobody noticed it for 370 years.

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:40.180
Okay?

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:41.550
I don't know what's going on.

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:44.318
I don't know what they're
doing in those monasteries.

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:50.170
But it's in the Tengyur,
it's in Nagarjuna.

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:55.840
The line exists in the
carving of Nagarjuna

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:57.480
because he's quoting the verse here,

00:33:57.480 --> 00:33:59.390
so I went and found it there.

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:02.790
Thank God for club de oro,

00:34:02.790 --> 00:34:06.900
so I can go find it in
another, from India,

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:08.717
I found it from India.

00:34:08.717 --> 00:34:11.850
And the line was missing,
the whole line was missing.

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:13.320
The answer to the question,

00:34:13.320 --> 00:34:16.190
what'd you think sansara was anyway?

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:18.550
And then the answer was
wrong, the answer was missing,

00:34:18.550 --> 00:34:20.020
so here's the answer.

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:22.973
MYA NGAN 'DAS PA'ANG CI ZHIG BRTAG

00:34:28.788 --> 00:34:31.160
Here's BRTAG.

00:34:31.160 --> 00:34:33.118
Yeah, okay.

00:34:33.118 --> 00:34:34.930
MYA NGAN 'DAS PA'ANG CI ZHIG BRTAG

00:34:34.930 --> 00:34:38.360
What the hell did you
come up with in your mind

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:41.963
when you were wondering
what the hell was Sansara?

00:34:42.979 --> 00:34:44.610
CI ZHIG BRTAG

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:47.670
What did you come up
with when I asked you?

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:49.870
We're talking about Nirvana, right,

00:34:49.870 --> 00:34:51.710
which is freedom from sansara,

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:53.930
but when I ask you what
the heck is sansara,

00:34:53.930 --> 00:34:56.530
you start wandering around BRTAG,

00:34:56.530 --> 00:35:00.486
a dumb parīkṣā (pariksha).

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:04.520
You went the wrong way in
your examination, got it?

00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:09.520
You did a "estúpido" pariksha.

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:12.570
Pariksha estúpido [laughs].

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:14.327
You did a dumb pariksha.

00:35:15.439 --> 00:35:17.030
Okay, got it, you guys?

00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:18.300
That's the missing line.

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:21.958
Now maybe it was impolite and
he didn't want to have it.

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:27.180
Let's look at the picture here.

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:30.196
If you can explain the picture to me.

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:34.250
It's a roulette wheel.

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:36.190
What's the point?

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:38.300
In your pursuit of Nirvana...

00:35:40.910 --> 00:35:44.038
[student responding indistinctly]

00:35:44.038 --> 00:35:47.253
In your pursuit of Nirvana, you are?

00:35:51.119 --> 00:35:55.286
[student responding indistinctly]

00:35:57.610 --> 00:36:00.390
You're spinning the wheel again [[aughs].

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:03.873
The way you pursue Nirvana,
spins sansara again.

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:07.527
Yeah, cool, good job [laughs].

00:36:08.490 --> 00:36:13.490
You're in pursuit of a Nirvana
for me spins sansara again.

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:15.560
Congratulations, you know?

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:20.200
Your pursuit of Nirvana
is spinning sansara again.

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:21.670
That's the point here.

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:24.130
And I would say, I
don't want to scare you,

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:28.360
but doing Medicine Buddha
mantras the wrong way

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:31.720
would make you sick.

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:35.163
Could sustain the suffering of your body.

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:37.940
Okay, I'm not saying you get cancer

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:39.635
from doing Medicine Buddha
mantras, I'm not saying that.

00:36:39.635 --> 00:36:43.790
But what I am saying is
it's possible to do them

00:36:45.208 --> 00:36:50.208
in a misunderstood way,
which would sustain illness.

00:36:51.300 --> 00:36:55.410
It would spin Sansara rather
than stopping the wheel.

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:58.500
That's heavy, that's heavy.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:03.330
We have to understand that
to make the mantras work,

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:07.350
we already have to protect
life, or the mantra won't work

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:09.950
and you will die while
you're doing mantras, got it?

00:37:15.318 --> 00:37:19.660
Okay, it's a good time,
yay, depressed time to go...

00:37:19.660 --> 00:37:21.670
Yes, question?

00:37:21.670 --> 00:37:24.748
- [Jigme] That would mean that it's better

00:37:24.748 --> 00:37:29.088
not to do any mantras at
all, which [indistinct]-

00:37:29.088 --> 00:37:31.005
- It's a good question.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:34.090
- Yeah, Geshe-la, does that mean

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:37.610
I should stop doing mantras if
I don't understand emptiness.

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:39.320
- No, you should keep doing mantras.

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:40.210
- Why?

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:42.610
- Because when you do
mantras you have, generally,

00:37:44.337 --> 00:37:48.360
you have a foundational sweet intent,

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:50.760
and that foundational sweet intent

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:54.570
will help you understand emptiness later.

00:37:54.570 --> 00:37:58.160
So if you don't understand
how the mantra works,

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:00.240
keep doing the mantra,
because it can help you

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:01.610
understand how the mantra works,

00:38:01.610 --> 00:38:05.990
but don't go too slow to
understand how the mantra works,

00:38:05.990 --> 00:38:07.730
because you will die first.

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:10.480
Really, seriously, no joke.

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:13.630
But yeah, anytime you do a
mantra, it's a good seed,

00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:15.730
maybe for a future life,

00:38:15.730 --> 00:38:19.220
if you don't work on
emptiness at the same time.

00:38:19.220 --> 00:38:21.120
So do your mantras for half an hour,

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:23.110
go read Nagarjuna for 10 minutes,

00:38:23.110 --> 00:38:25.170
come back to your
mantras for half an hour.

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:26.970
That's a good balance.

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:29.710
But don't ignore Nagarjuna and say

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:32.535
I'm going to sit all day and do mantras

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:35.140
because it's very possible you will die

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:38.500
before you get the
benefit from the mantras

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:40.030
or you help anybody.

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:45.030
So keep sprinkling in
the sugar of Nagarjuna,

00:38:45.840 --> 00:38:47.450
and don't say it's too hard for me

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:50.597
because sansara is hard and
you got to figure it out.

00:38:51.459 --> 00:38:54.243
Okay, all right, get to know Sanskrit.

00:38:54.243 --> 00:38:59.150
Yay, let's talk about sansara, sansara.

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:00.150
Hey, you got that?

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:03.450
Yeah, the pronunciation is saṃsāra.

00:39:03.450 --> 00:39:05.590
What's that note at the bottom?

00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:10.590
Please don't pronounce this as saṃsāra.

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:15.940
That's a mistake about
the M with a dot under it.

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:21.063
M with a dot under it, is not M.

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:26.310
They just didn't have
any other letter to use.

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:27.750
I'm sorry.

00:39:27.750 --> 00:39:32.750
So please, it's not M,
it's M with a dot under it.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:37.030
M with a dot under it means
like those French guys,

00:39:37.030 --> 00:39:40.240
like @(speaking in foreign language),

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:42.930
because they talk like this all the time.

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:45.090
Geshe-la you must come to Paris,

00:39:45.090 --> 00:39:49.390
you will have the best,
you know, it's that nasal.

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:52.762
So the M with a dot under
it just means [vocalizing].

00:39:52.762 --> 00:39:55.563
Sansara, sansara, don't say saMsara.

00:39:56.490 --> 00:40:00.400
It happened in, I don't know,
British made this mistake

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:02.080
and it got stuck.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:04.912
Everybody starts saying samsara, samsara.

00:40:04.912 --> 00:40:06.550
Sounds like a guys' name, right?

00:40:06.550 --> 00:40:09.163
Hey, meet my friend Sam Sara.

00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:10.933
Okay.

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:13.950
And it's especially irritating to me.

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:18.150
By the way, recently
somebody put out a video

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:21.980
with my picture in it and the
Sanskrit was all misspelled.

00:40:21.980 --> 00:40:23.630
Please don't do that.

00:40:23.630 --> 00:40:25.890
It makes me crazy.

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:28.940
So if you're going to use
my name and my picture,

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:32.040
please, please, please,
please let me check it first.

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:35.300
And somebody else had Tibetan
and it was all misspelled,

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:37.320
in the same time.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:40.720
So please, please, you
know, because I feel,

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:43.990
maybe you can see I feel
passionate about it.

00:40:45.134 --> 00:40:48.960
Okay, so Sri Lanka is misspelled,

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:52.010
Sivananda Yoga Centers is misspelled

00:40:52.010 --> 00:40:55.602
because they dropped the diacritic,

00:40:55.602 --> 00:40:58.470
they dropped the pronunciation sign

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:01.400
in the same way that the
dot got dropped from the M.

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:03.440
So here's this poor Mr. Sivananda,

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:06.840
whose name is mispronounced forever.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:11.050
And this poor country whose
name is mispronounced forever.

00:41:11.050 --> 00:41:15.516
It's Shri Lanka, it's not Sri Lanka.

00:41:15.516 --> 00:41:17.473
But the dot got dropped off.

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:19.253
Okay, got it?

00:41:19.253 --> 00:41:22.443
Same thing with samsara.

00:41:23.277 --> 00:41:26.123
Okay, Geshe-la, if you
want to irritate Geshe la,

00:41:26.990 --> 00:41:28.210
you know how.

00:41:28.210 --> 00:41:31.450
Okay, "sam" means to be
together, along with.

00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:34.029
By the way, by itself it's "sam",

00:41:34.029 --> 00:41:39.029
and then √sṛ means to flow,
and European is √ser also,

00:41:40.320 --> 00:41:45.320
and it's found in "serum",
which is a flowing stuff,

00:41:45.790 --> 00:41:50.580
and "strudel", which is
interesting, comes from √ser,

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:55.580
because of the whirlpool
look of a good cinnamon roll.

00:41:56.550 --> 00:41:59.450
Okay, and doesn't that make sense

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:02.663
for the Sanskrit of sansara?

00:42:03.540 --> 00:42:06.453
So strudel and sansara
are inextricably combined.

00:42:07.570 --> 00:42:09.590
If you ever have a strudel, give it to me,

00:42:09.590 --> 00:42:12.162
and I can take care of it

00:42:12.162 --> 00:42:14.323
because that's sansara, same root.

00:42:18.140 --> 00:42:21.710
That finishes, let's review what happened.

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:24.950
We were trying to find who is it

00:42:24.950 --> 00:42:27.100
that's going to get to Nirvana,

00:42:27.100 --> 00:42:30.820
and who is it that is chained
to their body and mind.

00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:33.390
And you guys got to think about it.

00:42:33.390 --> 00:42:36.660
Everyone says I want to get to Nirvana,

00:42:36.660 --> 00:42:39.630
everybody says I want to
escape the wheel of life.

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:42.650
Did you notice the houses
in the wheel of life?

00:42:42.650 --> 00:42:44.573
Did you notice the two guys in the boat

00:42:44.573 --> 00:42:45.552
in the wheel of life?

00:42:45.552 --> 00:42:49.141
Who's the two guys in the
boat in the wheel of life?

00:42:49.141 --> 00:42:50.179
[student responding indistinctly]

00:42:50.179 --> 00:42:52.442
MING GZUGS

00:42:52.442 --> 00:42:55.090
[students responding indistinctly]

00:42:55.090 --> 00:42:57.903
Yeah, body and mind, it's body and mind.

00:42:59.050 --> 00:43:00.890
When you get out of the wheel,

00:43:00.890 --> 00:43:04.190
you're going to have to
leave behind the boat

00:43:04.190 --> 00:43:05.800
with the body and mind in it.

00:43:05.800 --> 00:43:08.720
Well then who's left [laughs]?

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:11.010
Are you going to go around
without a body and mind?

00:43:11.010 --> 00:43:12.400
- Here, meet my friend.

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:13.233
- I can't see him.

00:43:13.233 --> 00:43:15.530
- Yeah, sorry, he don't got a body.

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:17.670
- Well, what does he think
about Democrats and Republicans?

00:43:17.670 --> 00:43:19.770
- Well, he doesn't think,
he doesn't have a mind.

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:22.114
He left that behind in the wheel of life.

00:43:22.114 --> 00:43:23.974
- Well, where is he?

00:43:23.974 --> 00:43:25.780
- I don't know.

00:43:25.780 --> 00:43:27.490
- You got to think about it.

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:30.390
You keep saying you want to
go to Nirvana, what's that?

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:32.890
I want to dump this body,
which is going to get cancer

00:43:32.890 --> 00:43:35.373
